  "1-3-12-rate-these-names": {
    "lessonTitle": "1.3.12 Rate These Names",
    "prompt": "Everything you need is in 1.3.6, 1.3.7, 1.3.8 and the worked example in 1.3.10: go back and reread them if you get stuck. A sentence per name is plenty, in your own everyday words.\n\nHere are six variable names. For each one, say whether it is a good name or a bad one, and why: name the rule it follows or breaks. (1.3.6, 1.3.7 and 1.3.8)\n\n  let x = 5;\n  let numberOfStudents = 5;\n  let d = \"Monday\";\n  let currentDay = \"Monday\";\n  let temp = 98.6;\n  let bodyTemperature = 98.6;\n\nThen answer two more:\n\n7. One of those six is genuinely arguable rather than clearly good or clearly bad. Which one, and what makes it arguable? (1.3.8)\n\n8. Match each of these four names: temp, x, a1, myVariable, to its problem: too vague, a single letter, a numbered name that says nothing, and meaningless but harmless-sounding. (1.3.7 and 1.3.8)",
    "rubric": [
      {
        "id": "all-six-judged",
        "title": "All six names are judged",
        "description": "Student gives a verdict on each of the six names, and the verdicts broadly land in the expected place: x bad, numberOfStudents good, d bad, currentDay good, temp borderline-or-weak, bodyTemperature good. Credit any answer that addresses all six, even if a call or two beyond temp is debatable rather than a clean match - the point is that they engaged with each name and reasoned through most of them, not that every verdict landed exactly right.",
        "points": 0
      },
      {
        "id": "reasons-cite-rules",
        "title": "Reasons name the rule, not just the verdict",
        "description": "For the bad names the student gives some reason grounded in the module's vocabulary - a single letter carries no information, the name is too vague, you can't tell what it holds, or something in that spirit even loosely phrased. Deny only bare verdicts ('bad', 'good') with literally no reason given, or 'it is confusing' repeated with nothing else attached.",
        "points": 0
      },
      {
        "id": "borderline-identified",
        "title": "Q7: identifies temp as the borderline one, with an argument",
        "description": "Student names temp and gives some reason it is arguable rather than simply bad - that it is a common short form, that it could be read more than one way, that experienced programmers would recognise it. Any real reason counts, however informally put. Credit naming temp with a reason; do not require them to weigh it against bodyTemperature.",
        "points": 0
      },
      {
        "id": "matching-correct",
        "title": "Q8: the four problems are matched correctly",
        "description": "Expected matching: temp = too vague; x = a single letter; a1 = numbered name that says nothing; myVariable = meaningless but harmless-sounding. Also accept the defensible swap of temp and x, i.e. temp described as an abbreviation with no context or too generic and x described as too vague or generic, since 1.3.7 and 1.3.8 use these words loosely. Accept any phrasing that captures the same idea, even loosely worded. Credit three or four correct; two or fewer is a genuine miss on the matching itself, not just phrasing.",
        "points": 0
      }
    ],
    "model": "glm-5.3-flash:cloud",
    "contextDocs": []
  },
