  "1-4-23-a1-4-1-two-languages": {
    "lessonTitle": "1.4.23 Two Languages, Two Purposes",
    "prompt": "1.4.2 and the 1.4.21 video cover why languages are built for different jobs; 1.4.7, 1.4.8, 1.4.15, 1.4.18 and 1.4.19 cover the paradigms. Go back and reread or rewatch if you get stuck. Your own everyday words throughout: do not copy descriptions from the internet, and you are not expected to have written code in either language.\n\nPick two programming languages other than JavaScript. 1.4.2 lists several, and you may pick others.\n\nFor each of your two languages, answer all three:\n1. What is this language typically used for? Name a specific kind of software, not just \"programs\". (1.4.2)\n2. Which paradigm or paradigms does it support: procedural, object-oriented, functional? You may name more than one. If your language fits none of the three well (SQL, for example), say in your own words how it works instead. (1.4.7 and 1.4.19)\n3. Why does that paradigm suit that kind of work? This is the important part. Connect the way the language organises a solution to the kind of problem it is usually pointed at. (1.4.8, 1.4.15 and 1.4.18)\n\nThen close with one sentence: name one thing that would make you pick a different language than either of these for a job.",
    "rubric": [
      {
        "id": "two-languages-named",
        "title": "Two specific non-JavaScript languages are named",
        "description": "Student names two distinct, real programming languages, neither of which is JavaScript. HTML and CSS do not count as programming languages and don't earn this criterion on their own; SQL does count. Credit any real language named clearly enough to identify - a common nickname or informal name is fine. Only withhold credit if just one language is actually discussed, or if a 'language' named is so vague it can't be identified as any real one ('a systems language').",
        "points": 0
      },
      {
        "id": "typical-use-specific",
        "title": "Each language's typical use is specific",
        "description": "For both languages the student names a concrete-ish kind of software or task - 'analysing data sets', 'iPhone apps', 'operating systems', 'querying a database'. Be generous about what counts as concrete: a plausible, recognizable use case in the student's own words is enough, even if broad ('building websites' counts). Credit both when covered; if one language's use is specific and the other is generic filler ('making programs', 'websites and stuff'), credit it as partial rather than missing.",
        "points": 0
      },
      {
        "id": "paradigm-named",
        "title": "A paradigm is named for each language",
        "description": "For both languages the student names at least one paradigm from the course vocabulary - procedural, object-oriented, or functional - and the pairing is defensible. Credit a reasonable pairing generously, even if incomplete or imprecisely worded (e.g. calling Python object-oriented without also saying it is multi-paradigm). If a paradigm is missing for one language but present and reasonable for the other, credit as partial rather than missing. Only withhold full credit where a pairing is plainly wrong (e.g. 'SQL is object-oriented'). The course teaches only procedural, object-oriented and functional, so for a language that fits none of them (SQL) accept any honest plain-words description of how it works, such as 'you describe the rows you want and the database finds them', as the paradigm; do not require a term the course has not taught.",
        "points": 0
      },
      {
        "id": "fit-explained",
        "title": "The fit between paradigm and purpose is explained, not just asserted",
        "description": "This is the core of the assignment. For at least one language, the student gives a reason connecting the paradigm to the kind of work - for example that a database query suits SQL because you describe the rows you want and don't care how they're found, or that game and app code suits objects because there are many similar things each keeping their own state. Be generous about how the reasoning is worded - a rough, intuitive version of the connection counts, it doesn't need textbook precision. Only withhold credit if the answer purely asserts the pairing with nothing at all connecting it to the kind of work ('Python is object-oriented and that fits data science').",
        "points": 0
      },
      {
        "id": "own-words",
        "title": "Written in the student's own words",
        "description": "The writing reads as a beginner's own understanding, with personal phrasing and natural variation. Be generous - uneven or clunky phrasing is expected and fine. Only withhold credit if it clearly reads as copied marketing copy or encyclopedia text: polished, uniform, and using terminology well beyond anything else in the submission.",
        "points": 0
      }
    ],
    "model": "glm-5.3-flash:cloud",
    "contextDocs": []
  },
