  "7-1-2-q2-reflection": {
    "lessonTitle": "8.1.2 Q2 Reflection",
    "prompt": "This one is entirely about the game you built, so there is no reading to look up: the answers come from your own project and your own memory of building it. A few sentences per answer is plenty, in your own everyday words. There is no wrong answer here as long as you are describing your actual project honestly.\n\n1. Your game changed between the pitch in your README and the version you handed in. Name one thing that changed, and explain what made you change it.\n\n2. Describe one bug that took you more than a few minutes to find. How did you track it down?\n\n3. The checklist gave you twelve requirements and no design. Which requirement was hardest to fit into YOUR game idea, and what did you do about it?",
    "rubric": [
      {
        "id": "design-changed",
        "title": "Names a specific change from pitch to shipped, and what caused it",
        "description": "Student names something concrete that differs between their README pitch and the game they handed in, with some reason behind it, however brief - playtesting, scope, something that wasn't fun or didn't work. A short, real, named change with a one-phrase reason counts; 'the enemies chased the player and it was impossible, so I made them patrol instead' is the clean case, but a thinner version of the same shape still counts. Deny only fully generic non-answers like 'I changed some stuff' or 'I added more features' that don't name anything real.",
        "points": 0
      },
      {
        "id": "debugging-account",
        "title": "Gives a real account of tracking down one bug",
        "description": "Student describes a specific bug and something about how they tracked it down, however briefly - printing values, commenting code out, reading an error, checking one thing at a time. A short, real account counts even if thin; deny only a response that names a bug but skips the hunt entirely ('my sprite wouldn't move, then I fixed it'), or a bug so vague a reader couldn't picture it at all.",
        "points": 0
      },
      {
        "id": "requirement-tension",
        "title": "Explains fitting a required feature into their own design",
        "description": "Student identifies which checklist requirement was hardest to justify inside their particular game and says something about the decision they made - reworking the design to need it, or finding a use that fits - however briefly. This is about design judgment, not difficulty of coding, so 'joints were hard to code' still doesn't earn it, but a thinner version of 'my game had nothing to save so I added a high score table' does.",
        "points": 0
      }
    ],
    "model": "glm-5.3-flash:cloud",
    "contextDocs": []
  },
